DEBUG:openai._base_client:Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'timeout': 600.0, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-983b5f8c-f772-4842-a7ff-a882dd902f9c', 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'From the text below, extract the following entities in the following format:\n\nassays: <semicolon-separated list of every experimental assay the authors performed, each by the name the paper uses for it (for example: flow cytometry; ELISA; RNA sequencing; plaque reduction neutralization test)>\n\n\nText:\n580 • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • Vahey et al\nM A J O R A R T I C L E\nExpression of Genes Associated with\nImmunoproteasome Processing of Major\nHistocompatibility Complex Peptides Is Indicative\nof Protection with Adjuvanted RTS,S Malaria Vaccine\nMaryanne T. Vahey,1 Zhining Wang,2 Kent E. Kester,1 James Cummings,1 D. Gray Heppner, Jr,1 Martin E. Nau,2\nOpokua Ofori-Anyinam,4 Joe Cohen,4 Thierry Coche,4 W. Ripley Ballou,3 and Christian F. Ockenhouse1\n1The Walter Reed Army Institute of Research, Silver Spring, and 2The Henry M. Jackson Foundation for the Advancement of Military Medicine,\nRockville, Maryland; 3The Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, Seattle, Washington; 4GlaxoSmithKline Biologicals, Rixensart, Belgium\nBackground.\nPatterns of expressed genes in the peripheral blood mononuclear cells of persons who were\nreceiving RTS,S/AS01 or RTS,S/AS02 malaria vaccine and were undergoing experimental challenge with mosquito-\nborne falciparum malaria were examined to identify markers associated with protection.\nMethods.\nThirty-nine vaccine recipients were assessed at study entry; on the day of the third vaccination; at\n24 h, 72 h, and 2 weeks after vaccination; and on day 5 after challenge. Of 39 vaccine recipients, 13 were protected\nand 26 were not. Eleven vaccine recipients exhibited delayed onset of parasitemia. All infectivity control subjects\ndeveloped parasitemia. Prediction analysis of microarrays identiﬁed genes corresponding with protection. Gene\nset enrichment analysis identiﬁed sets of genes associated with protection after the third vaccination and before\nchallenge.\nResults.\nAfter the third vaccination and before challenge, differential expression of genes in the immunopro-\nteasome pathway distinguished protected and nonprotected persons. At 5 days after challenge, differential expression\nof genes associated with programmed cell death distinguished between subjects protected and not protected from\nmalaria blood-stage infection.\nConclusions.\nThe up-regulation of genes associated with the efﬁcient processing of major histocompatibility\ncomplex peptides suggests a potential role of the vaccine in conferring major histocompatibility complex class 1–\nmediated protection and may represent a useful surrogate marker of vaccine efﬁcacy without the need for challenge.\nThe RTS,S is a subunit recombinant vaccine expressed\nin yeast that represents the central repeat and C-ter-\nminal portion of the Plasmodium falciparum circum-\nsporozoite protein (CSP) covalently linked with the S\nantigen of hepatitis B virus [1]. The vaccine, developed\nthrough collaboration between the Walter Reed Army\nInstitute of Research and GlaxoSmithKline Biologicals\n[2], is the only vaccine shown to prevent malaria con-\nsistently in a portion of vaccine recipients, as evaluated\nby mosquito challenge [3–7]. Numerous formulations\nReceived 17 July 2009; accepted 29 September 2009; electronically published\n19 January 2010.\nReprints or correspondence: Maryanne T. Vahey, Walter Reed Army Institute of\nResearch, 503 Robert Grant Ave, Silver Spring, MD 20403 (maryanne.vahey@us\n.army.mil).\nThe Journal of Infectious Diseases\n2010;201:580–9\n 2010 by the Infectious Diseases Society of America. All rights reserved.\n0022-1899/2010/20104-0015$15.00\nDOI: 10.1086/650310\nof the RTS,S antigen in various adjuvants have been\ndeveloped and evaluated in nonhuman primates [8–10]\nand in malaria-naive volunteers [1, 11], to improve\nimmunogenicity and efﬁcacy so that they approach the\ncurrent overall protective efﬁcacy (∼50%) of RTS,S/\nASO1B in malaria-naive adults [7].\nThe immunologic response of malaria-naive adults\nto vaccination with adjuvanted RTS,S shows signiﬁcant\nindividual variation: some vaccine recipients are pro-\nPotential conﬂicts of interest: O.O-A., J.C., T.C., and W.R.B. are or have been\nemployees of and own stock in GlaxoSmithKline Biologicals, the manufacturer of\nthe vaccines described in this article. All other authors: no conﬂicts reported.\nFinancial support: US Army Medical Research and Materiel Command and the\nHenry M. Jackson Foundation for the Advancement of Military Medicine\n(cooperative agreement W81XWH-04-2-0005); Military Infectious Disease Research\nProgram; and the US Army Medical Materiel and Development Activity, Ft. Detrick,\nMaryland.\nThe opinions or assertions contained herein are the private views of the authors,\nand are not to be construed as ofﬁcial or as reﬂecting the views of the Department\nof the Army or the Department of Defense.\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\nDifferential Expression and Protection • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • 581\ntected, and among those who are not protected, some exhibit\na delay in the development of parasitemia [1, 3–7, 11]. Pro-\ntection of malaria-naive volunteers is associated with both CSP-\nspeciﬁc antibody and T cell responses. CSP-speciﬁc CD4+ T\ncells associated with interleukin (IL)–2, interferon (IFN)–g,\ntumor necrosis factor (TNF)–a, and CD40L; anti-CSP im-\nmunoglobulin (Ig) G levels; and ex vivo CSP-speciﬁc IFN-g\nenzyme-linked immunospots are observed in persons protected\nfrom malaria after challenge with infected mosquitoes [7, 12].\nThese immunologic markers approximate, but do not accu-\nrately predict, which of the vaccine recipients will be fully pro-\ntected, will be unprotected, or will show a delay in disease onset.\nThe spectrum of the immunologic and protective response\nto adjuvanted RTS,S offers an opportunity to decipher host\ngenomic mechanisms that might account for differences in pro-\ntection observed after experimental challenge. At the host ge-\nnomic level, assessment of the divergent responses to vacci-\nnation with adjuvanted RTS,S has the potential to elucidate the\nfundamental features of immune responses to vaccination that\nresult in protection and that might be exploited to inform\ndevelopment of a more protective malaria vaccine.\nGene expression proﬁling has being successfully used to in-\nvestigate host genetic responses and has been applied to predict\nthe responses to treatment and clinical outcome associated with\nseveral diseases, including cancer [13], HIV disease [14], tu-\nberculosis [15], and multiple sclerosis [16]. Gene chip–based\nprediction strategies have received approval from the US Food\nand Drug Administration as prognostic tools for breast cancer\n[17–22]. The gene expression proﬁles of PBMCs collected be-\nfore and after immunization with a yellow fever attenuated\nvaccine have revealed hundreds of genes that are induced and\nsuppressed temporally in response to immunization and that\npredict polyfunctional immune responses [23, 24].\nThe goals of the present study were to determine whether\nthere is a gene expression proﬁle that is predictive of an in-\ndividual’s response to the adjuvanted RTS,S malaria vaccine\nand to identify the time at which that proﬁle can be discerned\nbefore challenge. Using global gene expression proﬁling, we\nidentiﬁed expression of genes in the immunoproteasome path-\nway that impinge on the efﬁciency of MHC class 1 processing\nand subsequent antigen presentation, and that are differentially\nexpressed in persons protected by the vaccine and are discerned\nbefore human challenge with the pathogen.\nSUBJECTS, MATERIALS, AND METHODS\nClinical specimens.\nPBMCs were obtained after informed\nconsent was provided by study volunteers enrolled in a phase\n2b trial of adjuvanted RTS,S [7]. The RTS,S vaccine used in\nthis study consists of sequences of the P. falciparum CSP ex-\npressed in hepatitis B surface antigen and formulated with pro-\nprietary adjuvant systems AS01 (MPL, QS21, and liposomes)\nor AS02 (MPL, QS21, and an oil-in-water emulsion) [25]. Chal-\nlenge was performed with a homologous 3D7 strain of P. fal-\nciparum delivered by 5 bites from infected mosquitoes [26]. To\nassess the efﬁciency of the challenge, 12 persons without pre-\nvious vaccination were also challenged. Infection with malaria\nwas determined by detection of parasite in malaria thick blood\nsmears during the 30 days after challenge. Protection was de-\nﬁned as failure to develop P. falciparum asexual-stage parasi-\ntemia. Partial protection or delay was deﬁned as a signiﬁcant\nprolongation in the prepatent period (48 h), compared with\nthat noted in the infectivity control subjects [27]. PBMCs were\ncollected by gradient centrifugation in cell preparation tubes\n(Becton Dickinson) and cryopreserved. Samples obtained from\nvaccine recipients were assessed for gene expression patterns at\nstudy entry (36 samples); on the day of the third vaccination\n(44 samples); at 24 h (43 samples), 72 h (43 samples), and 2\nweeks (37 samples) thereafter; and at day 5 after challenge (39\nsamples). For infectivity controls, samples were collected at\nentry and at 5 days post challenge.\nPreparation of samples for GeneChip analysis.\nPreparation\nof cellular RNA and subsequent processing for GeneChip analysis\nwere performed as described elsewhere [28], by use of the HG-\nU133 A 2.0 and HG-U133 Plus 2.0 GeneChips (Affymetrix) and\nthe Agilent 2100 Bioanalyzer system (Agilent Technologies), to\nassess the integrity and quantity of RNA.\nGeneChip quality control and data normalization.\nGene-\nChips with a scaling factor 150 and an array outlier percentage\n15% on dChip software (2005 version) [29] were eliminated\nfrom further analysis. Genes scored as absent in all samples by\nuse of GeneChip operating software (GCOS) were eliminated\nfrom analysis. Only fully annotated gene targets were assessed.\nCEL ﬁles were normalized at the probe level using the Robust\nMulti-chip Average (RMA) method [30] built into the Bio-\nConductor package Affy-1.12.2. RMA is a method of normal-\nization of microarray data that leverages gene expression as-\nsessments made on Affymetrix gene chips. For each probe set,\nthe technique generates an estimated value for the probe and\nchip effects that result in the overall pattern of probe set values\nobserved in the data set. RMA is comprised of 3 calculation\napplications that address background correction, normalization\nof quantiles, and median polish summarization. Data sets were\ngenerated on Affymetrix U133A 2.0 and Plus 2.0 GeneChips\nby use of 11-micron technology, with the former being a subset\nof the latter and thus facilitating normalization between data\nsets. A 2-step approach using RMA was used to normalize data\nsets from these 2 platforms. Data sets from the U133A 2.0 and\nPlus 2.0 gene chips were ﬁrst normalized individually. Subse-\nquently, the common probe sets were identiﬁed and normalized\nusing the quantiles calculation in the Affy package (Bio-\nConductor) to generate the ﬁnal normalized data set for further\nanalysis. The complete portfolio of data sets supporting the\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\n582 • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • Vahey et al\nFigure 1.\nStrong but transitory changes in genes associated with inﬂammatory processes are associated with vaccination with adjuvanted RTS,S.\nHeat map of genes associated with inﬂammatory responses in all vaccine recipients in the study on the day of the third vaccination and at 24 h and\n72 h after vaccination. Sixty-three unique genes from inﬂammatory response pathways are signiﬁcantly (false discovery rate [FDR], !1%) up-regulated\n24 h after the third vaccination. Up-regulation of these genes resolves by 72 h after vaccination. Genes associated with apoptosis and the protein\nkinase cascade follow a similar pattern. Gene expression values are z-score transformed (z p [Xmean]/standard deviation) across samples. A\npositive z score (red) and a negative z score (green) denote up- and down-regulation, respectively. The color gradient ranges from a z score of 2.0\nto 2.0.\nTable 1.\nGene Annotations for Signiﬁcance\nAnalysis of Microarrays (SAM; Version 3.0) Re-\nported in Figure 1\nThe table is available in its entirety in the online\nversion of the Journal of Infectious Diseases\nanalyses in this study is available at Gene Expression Omnibus\n(available\nat:\nhttp://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/;\naccession\nnumber GSE18323).\nDifferential expression.\nDifferentially expressed genes were\nidentiﬁed using the statistical program Signiﬁcance Analysis of\nMicroarrays (SAM; version 3.0) [31], and cluster analysis of\nmicroarray data sets was performed using the MultiExperiment\nViewer (version 4.1.01; available at http://www.jcvi.org.cms\n.research.software). SAM identiﬁes genes whose expression has\nsigniﬁcantly changed, by leveraging a set of gene-speciﬁc t tests.\nGenes are assigned a score derived from the change in expres-\nsion relative to the standard deviation for all measurements\nmade for that gene. The percentage of genes considered to be\nsigniﬁcant by chance is deﬁned by the false discovery rate\n(FDR). For SAM, we used the cutoff FDR !1%, which is very\nstringent. Principal component analysis, which is an eigen vec-\ntor–based multivariate tool that reveals the most informative\ntrends in complex data sets by identifying the predominant\ngradients in data sets, was performed using Partek Pro (version\n6.0; Partek).\nDerivation of gene ontology and functional associations.\nFunctions and biological classiﬁcations of differentially ex-\npressed genes were analyzed using the Web-based tool Data-\nbase for Annotated Visualization and Integrated Discovery\n(DAVID), which sorts gene lists into functional proﬁles by use\nof gene ontology categories [32]. We used DAVID gene on-\ntology/biological processes level 5 (GOTEMR_BP_5) for our\nanalyses, with a P value of .05.\nClassiﬁcation.\nClass prediction was performed using pre-\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\nDifferential Expression and Protection • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • 583\nFigure 2.\nThe effect of vaccination and challenge on the global host\nresponse, as illustrated by principal components analysis of differentially\nexpressed genes. Red spheres denote 36 RTS,S vaccine recipients at\nbaseline before vaccination and challenge (group A). Blue spheres denote\n39 volunteers who are vaccinated and then challenged (group B), and\ngreen spheres denote 12 control nonvaccinated volunteers who were\nchallenged (group C). The separation of group A and group B reﬂects the\ncombined effect of vaccination and challenge. The separation of group\nA and group C reﬂects the effects of challenge and challenge only. The\nseparation of group B and group C indicates that volunteers who are\nvaccinated (group B) respond to challenge differently from those who are\nnot vaccinated (group C).\ndiction analysis of microarrays with R (PAM-R; available at\nhttp://www.bioconductor.org), which implements the nearest\nshrunken centroid algorithm [33]. PAM-R uses a k-fold, iter-\native cross-validation method to estimate the predictive capa-\nbility of a set of “classiﬁer” genes that together are capable of\nassigning samples to a given group.\nGene set enrichment analysis.\nGene set enrichment analy-\nsis (GSEA) or R-GSEA and the Molecular Signatures Database\nwere used to identify differentially expressed sets of genes. The\nsoftware and gene set database are available from The Broad\nInstitute of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and Har-\nvard University (available at: http://www.broadinstitute.org).\nGSEA identiﬁes the sets of genes, as opposed to individual\ngenes, associated with biological pathways that are coregulated.\nWe used the GSEA’s default statistical cutoff of FDR !0.25.\nData analysis algorithm.\nSequential steps in the analysis\nof differential gene expression data in this study were performed\nas follows. After RMA normalization and before SAM, probe\nsets that were “absent” in all samples were excluded. SAM was\nused to identify differentially expressed genes. The parameters\nfor SAM analysis used in this study were FDR: !1%; data type:\n2-class unpaired; arrays centered: FALSE; minimum fold\nchange: no limit; input data: log 2 scale; number of neighbors\nfor K nearest neighbors (KNN): 10; and number of permu-\ntations: 100. The differentially expressed genes identiﬁed by\nSAM were further annotated by DAVID at gene ontology/bi-\nological processes level 5 (GOTEMR_BP_5) (\n).\nP p .05\nReal-time polymerase chain reaction.\nTo conﬁrm obser-\nvations from the gene chip data, TaqMan Gene Expression\nAssays (Applied Biosystems) were used to detect genes in the\nimmunoproteosome pathway (PSME2, PSMB9, PSMB6, and\nPSMA4) in 3 randomly chosen samples from protected and\nnonprotected persons, at 2 weeks after the third vaccination.\nThe ﬂuorescence signals were measured in real time with the\nuse of ABI HT7900, and critical threshold values were generat-\ned using the software SDS2.1 (Applied Biosystems). Glyceral-\ndehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase was used as an internal\ncontrol to calculate fold changes in target genes in samples ob-\ntained from protected versus nonprotected persons, by use of\nthe 2DDCT method [34].\nRESULTS\nCharacteristics of the study groups.\nInfectivity control sub-\njects and vaccine recipients did not differ with respect to sex,\nrace, or age. For the purposes of this analysis, assessment of\nprotection against malaria infection after challenge included\nsamples from subjects in the study who were receiving an ad-\njuvanted RTS,S vaccine.\nChallenge outcome.\nThirty-nine vaccine recipients com-\npleted the study, 13 (33%) of whom were protected and 26\n(67%) of whom were not protected after challenge. Of the 26\nvaccine recipients who were not protected, 11 (28%) had a\ndelayed onset of parasitemia indicating a signiﬁcant effect on\npreerythrocytic development, and 15 vaccine recipients devel-\noped parasitemia without delay.\nAssociation of differential gene expression after the third\nvaccination with strong, transitory changes in gene expression.\nSAM (version 3) using the day of the third vaccination as\nbaseline, with an FDR of 1%, was used to identify genes that\nexhibited a signiﬁcant change in expression level at 24 h and\n72 h after the third vaccination (\nsubjects).\nn p 43\nFigure 1 is a heat map showing the changes in expression\nof 63 genes associated with inﬂammatory responses on the day\nof the third vaccination and at 24 h and 72 h later. These genes\nare markedly up-regulated 24 h after vaccination; however, by\n72 h after vaccination, they resolve to the levels of expression\nobserved on the day of the third vaccination. Seventy-nine\ngenes in the protein kinase cascade family and 107 genes in\nthe apoptosis family follow the same pattern at 24 h and 72 h\nafter the third vaccination. Details related to these genes are\npresented in Table 1.\nDistinct global expression patterns in vaccine-naive persons\nunchallenged and challenged with or without vaccination.\nWe hypothesized that, regardless of protection status, the re-\nsponses of the host to challenge would be different for indi-\nviduals who were vaccinated and those who were vaccine naive.\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\n584 • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • Vahey et al\nFigure 3.\nPrediction analysis of microarrays with R (PAM-R) classiﬁcation analysis of outcome at 5 days after challenge. A, PAM-R analysis with\n100% accuracy in classiﬁcation by the 393 genes in the classiﬁer set. Patients 1–13 belong to the protected group, patients 14–24 belong to the\ngroup with delayed onset of disease, and patients 25–39 belong to the nonprotected group. Each patient is denoted by an X, a triangle, and a plus\nsign, which indicate the probability (as shown on the y-axis) of a patient being classiﬁed in the protected group, the delayed group, and the nonprotected\ngroup, respectively. For a given patient, the sum of all probabilities is always equal to 1. B, A heat map representing the expression patterns of the\n393-gene classiﬁer in protected, delayed, and nonprotected patients. Gene expression values are z-score transformed (z p [Xmean]/standard deviation)\nacross samples. A positive z score (red) and a negative z score (green) denote up- and down-regulation, respectively. The color gradient ranges from\na z score of 2.0 to 2.0. C, A pie chart of those functional categories in the classiﬁer that are annotated by DAVID at biological process level 5.\nGenes associated with progression through the cell cycle and the regulation of apoptosis are predominant. Only categories for which\nare\nP ! .05\nshown.\nWe assessed global expression proﬁles from vaccine-naive per-\nsons, samples taken at day 5 after challenge of persons who\nhad received vaccine and of persons who were challenged but\nhad not received vaccine. The principal component analysis\nshown in Figure 2 shows 461 up-regulated genes and 626 down-\nregulated genes (identiﬁed at an FDR !1%) in persons chal-\nlenged and infected without vaccination. Challenge after vac-\ncination results in up-regulation of 6219 genes and down-reg-\nulation of 5670 genes, suggesting an extensive and broader host\nresponse associated with vaccination.\nClassiﬁcation/prediction analysis of protection at day 5 af-\nter challenge sorts samples into protected, not protected, and\ndelayed onset of disease categories with 100% accuracy.\nPAM-\nR was used to identify sets of classiﬁer genes capable of sorting\nsamples (\nsubjects) into protected, not protected, and\nn p 39\ndelayed onset of disease categories, on day 5 after challenge.\nFigure 3A shows the results of PAM-R for these groups. Figure\n3B shows expression levels of 393 genes in the classiﬁer set for\nvaccine recipients who are protected, delayed in the onset of\nparasitemia, and not delayed in the onset of parasitemia groups.\nA pie chart of the known functional categories of 393 genes\ncomprising the 100% accurate classiﬁer set that delineates these\n2 outcome groups is shown in Figure 3C.\nGenes in the classiﬁer set are those that can be annotated by\nDAVID and for which\n. Those associated with both pos-\nP ! .05\nitive and negative regulation of progression through the cell\ncycle and with control of apoptosis and programmed cell death\nare predominant.\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\nDifferential Expression and Protection • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • 585\nFigure 4.\nGene set enrichment analysis (GSEA) identiﬁcation of genes in the immunoproteasome pathway associated with the processing of major\nhistocompatibility complex class 1 peptides and differentially up-regulated in persons protected by RTS,S vaccination A, Running enrichment score\n(RES) showing that the proteasome degradation pathway is up-regulated in the protected group. There are 32 genes in proteasome degradation\npathway (each vertical bar denotes 1 gene). If this pathway were not associated with protection, these 32 genes (32 vertical bars) would distribute\naround the center or would be evenly distributed from left to right. Because these 32 genes are apparently biased toward the right-hand side, this\npathway is associated with protection. The y-axis displays the enrichment score, which peaks at 0.596. B, Permutation test of the signiﬁcance of\nthe proteasome degradation pathway. The GSEA algorithm executes a permutation test to calculate the nominal P value and false discovery rate\n(FDR) of identiﬁed pathways. After multiple test correction, the FDR of the proteasome degradation pathway is 0.086, which is less than the default\ncutoff of 0.25. C, A heat map showing the up-regulation of core enrichment genes in the immunoproteasome pathway in the protected groups. The\nexpression values are z score normalized by GSEA. Red and blue denote up- and down-regulation, respectively. At the bottom are sample names.\nSamples belonging to the nonprotected group are designated by the preﬁx “N-,” and samples belonging to the protected group are designated by the\npre “P-.” Core enrichment genes are marked. ES, enrichment score; FWER, familywise-error rate; neg., negative; NES, normalized ES; nom., nominal;\nP(ES), null distribution density of ES; pos., positive.\nGSEA and conﬁrmation by reverse-transcriptase polymerase\nchain reaction.\nHigh-resolution GSEA was used to identify\nsets of genes that were differentially expressed at 2 weeks after\nthe third vaccination but before challenge (\nsubjects).\nn p 37\nFigure 4A shows the enrichment of genes in the proteasome\ndegradation pathway that are up-regulated in persons in the\nprotected group. Vertical black bars denote the 32 genes in the\nproteasome degradation pathway. Bars are concentrated toward\nthe right side of the graph, indicating that their expression is\nup-regulated in the protected group. Figure 4B shows the gene\nset null distribution analysis, which indicates that the genes in\nthe proteasome degradation pathway are statistically signiﬁcant\nat\n, with an FDR of 0.086. This was the only set of\nP ! .001\ngenes with both a signiﬁcant P value and a multiple test cor-\nrection FDR that was less than the default value of 0.25. Figure\n4C presents a heat map showing differential regulation of genes\nin the proteasome degradation pathway in the nonprotected\nand protected groups. The functional network of these genes\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\n586 • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • Vahey et al\nFigure 5.\nThe functional pathway of the genes in the immunoproteasome pathway identiﬁed by gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA), as up-regulated\nin persons protected by vaccination with RTS,S. The speciﬁc up-regulation of PSME2 (a component of the 11S regulator) in persons protected by the\nvaccine directs the formation of the immunoproteasome associated with major histocompatibility complex class 1 processing rather than that of the\nconstitutive proteasome associated with general protein degradation. The up-regulation of the expression of genes shown in red was independently\nconﬁrmed by real-time polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR), as is shown in Figure 6. 19S regulator, directs constitutive proteasome protein degradation;\nPSME2, component of 11S regulator that directs immunoproteasome function; PSMA4, proteasome (prosome, macropain) subunit, a type, 4; PSMB6,\nproteasome (prosome, macropain) subunit, b type, 6; PSMB9, proteasome (prosome, macropain) subunit, b type, 9.\nis shown in Figure 5. Genes whose expression was indepen-\ndently conﬁrmed by reverse-transcriptase polymerase chain re-\naction are highlighted in red. Genes of the immunoproteasome\ncomplex direct the synthesis of proteins that include a 20S core\nthat combines with a 19S regulator segment to form a consti-\ntutive proteasome complex known to degrade a wide range of\nproteins. The combination of the 20S core and the 11S regulator\nsegment forms the immunoproteasome that speciﬁcally pro-\ncesses peptides for presentation to the MHC complex. Up-\nregulation of PSME2 (a component of the 11S regulator),\nPSMA4, PSMB6, and PSMB9, as revealed by this analysis, in-\ndicates that the speciﬁc pathway responsible for differential\nresponse to the vaccine impinges on the processing of antigenic\npeptides.\nTo show the speciﬁcity of these observations to the vaccine,\ngene expression results from subjects at study entry were sub-\njected to GSEA. No gene sets reached an FDR of !0.25, indi-\ncating that the proteasome degradation pathway was not up-\nregulated in the protected group before vaccination.\nFigure 6A shows reverse-transcriptase polymerase chain re-\naction conﬁrmation of differential gene expression in 3 ran-\ndomly chosen samples obtained from protected and not pro-\ntected outcome groups at 2 weeks after the third vaccination\nand identiﬁed by expression data as genes associated with reg-\nulation of MHC processing in the immunoproteasome path-\nway. Four pivotal genes were assessed. The correlation coefﬁ-\ncient for concordance of these 2 independent techniques was\n, as shown in Figure 6B.\nr p 0.886\nDISCUSSION\nThe goal of this study was to ascertain whether distinct patterns\nof gene expression might be associated with outcome in a chal-\nlenge model of P. falciparum malaria in persons immunized\nwith a partially efﬁcacious vaccine. A second goal of the study\nwas to further reﬁne these patterns to identify speciﬁc biological\nprocesses that might account for the association. A serendip-\nitous accomplishment of the study was identiﬁcation of dis-\ncriminatory patterns as early as 2 weeks after the third vacci-\nnation and before challenge with the pathogen.\nGlobal patterns of gene expression, as shown by PCA, are\ndistinct in vaccinated subjects who had subsequently been chal-\nlenged with malaria sporozoites, compared with nonvaccinated\nsubjects who were challenged. The effect of vaccination is re-\nvealed in the robust differential gene expression pattern ob-\nserved 24 h after vaccination. SAM indicates that genes asso-\nciated with host inﬂammatory response, apoptosis, and the\nprotein kinase cascade are up-regulated within 24 h of the third\nvaccination in all vaccine recipients, regardless of the outcome\nafter challenge. This remarkably robust level of gene expression\nresolves by 72 h after vaccination and returns to the levels of\ngene expression observed before the third vaccination. These\nresponses are likely associated with host response to the vaccine\nand its antigenic components to include the CSPs, the hepatitis\nB surface antigen, and the adjuvant. Although not indicative\nof protection, the magnitude and temporal association of these\nresponses reveal the power of single gene expression patterns\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\nDifferential Expression and Protection • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • 587\nFigure 6.\nReverse-transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) conﬁrmation of the up-regulation of PSME2, PSMA4, PSMB6, and PSMB9 in\nprotected individuals. A, The fold change in gene expression (as shown on the y-axis) relative to the samples with the lowest expression value for\na given gene and assay. Four genes (PSME2, PSMB9, PSMB6, and PSMA4) are displayed along the x-axis. Triangles and stars denote samples obtained\nfrom protected and nonprotected individuals, respectively. Fold change values denoted by triangles are consistently greater than those denoted by\nstars, conﬁrming the up-regulation of these genes in protected individuals. B, The fold change in gene expression (as shown on the x-axis) detected\nby RT-PCR and the fold change (as shown on the y-axis) detected by Affymetrix GeneChip technology. The correlation coefﬁcient is 0.886.\nto reﬂect immune responses in the host after vaccination with\na recombinant subunit protein vaccine. Relatively small, het-\nerogeneous data sets, such as the ones used in the present study,\nare most amenable to classiﬁcation analysis with PAM-R, in\nwhich the goal is to identify genes associated with a known\nclinical outcome. Although the data set is small, and although\none cannot preclude the possibility that the observations may\nbe data set dependent, all 39 immunized subjects who under-\nwent malaria challenge can be correctly categorized as belonging\nto 1 of 3 outcome groups: individuals who were protected,\nthose who were not protected, and those who experienced a\ndelay in the onset of disease. Genes that underpin this classi-\nﬁcation include those associated with cell death and progression\nthrough the cell cycle. Although these patterns cannot be di-\nrectly associated with any speciﬁc cell type, because they are\ndrawn from the peripheral PBMC compartment, the 100%\naccuracy of the classiﬁcation strongly supports an association\nbetween these processes and the outcome after challenge.\nIt is also is important to consider our observations in light\nof the fact that genetic polymorphisms of the human leukocyte\nantigen system are known to signiﬁcantly affect immune re-\nsponses to vaccines. Because of the diversity of human leukocyte\nantigen (HLA) types and the limited number of persons in this\nphase 2b clinical trial for which partial HLA data have been\ngenerated, insufﬁcient statistical power prevents correlation of\nHLA type with the 3 outcome groups in our study. It will be\ncritical to include assessment of the HLA type of individuals\nparticipating in future, large-scale trials of the RTS,S vaccine.\nOur observations are made in the context of the RTS,S vaccine\nonly, and we cannot discern absolutely whether they are as-\nsociated with speciﬁc characteristics of either the RTS,S vaccine\nor the individual volunteers who failed to be protected by the\nRTS,S vaccine on challenge.\nIt could be argued that assessment of samples after challenge\ncould be inﬂuenced by the introduction of the pathogen. To\naddress this argument, we undertook assessment of gene ex-\npression patterns 2 weeks after the third vaccination but before\nchallenge. PAM-R analysis at this time point did not have the\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\n588 • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • Vahey et al\npower to resolve subtle changes in gene expression associated\nwith outcome prior to challenge. To identify the earliest and\nmost subtle changes in gene expression that could be speciﬁcally\nassociated with protection, high-resolution GSEA, which fur-\nther reﬁnes the patterns of expression by use of gene sets rather\nthan individual genes, was employed. GSEA performed on sam-\nples obtained 2 weeks after the third vaccination showed that\nup-regulation of genes in the proteasome degradation pathway,\nassociated with the efﬁcient processing of the MHC peptides,\nis associated with protection. That this observation is speciﬁc\nto the vaccine is borne out by the fact that no such association\nis observed in GSEA of the vaccine-naive individuals at study\nentry (data not shown).\nAlthough CD4+ T cells and CD8+ T cells were not assessed\nin our analysis of the total PBMC population, the genes iden-\ntiﬁed in the immunoproteasome pathway—PSME2, PSMB9,\nPSMB6, and PSMA4—play complex, pivotal roles in the efﬁ-\nciency of MHC peptide processing and subsequent antigen pre-\nsentation [35–37]. The prognostic signiﬁcance of immunopro-\nteosome gene expression was recently found to be associated\nwith the degree abnormal histopathologic grade in renal cell\ncarcinoma [38]. Low-level immunoproteasome subunit ex-\npression was associated with shortened duration of survival in\npatients with renal cell carcinoma, indicating that abnormal\nantigen processing/presentation leads to escape from immu-\nnologic surveillance. In our study, immunoproteasome control\nof the selective protein degradation necessary for MHC class I\nand class II antigen processing and the key role of IFN-g in\nenhancing this process are consistent with the observation that\nvaccine recipients with up-regulated expression of immuno-\nproteasome pathway genes are protected after challenge.\nIdentiﬁcation of a set of genes associated with immunopro-\nteasome processing of peptides for MHC presentation and pro-\ntection by a vaccine is a point of departure for studies of speciﬁc\nsubpopulations of cells that might conﬁrm and extend our\nobservations. The identiﬁcation of additional elements of the\nimmunoproteasome pathway and an evaluation of these genes\nand proteins as regulators, second messengers, and potential\npredictive biomarkers will contribute to the optimal design and\nassessment of vaccines and the further elucidation of the as-\nsociation between immunogenicity and protection.\nAcknowledgments\nWe thank our colleagues at GlaxoSmithKline Biologicals—Erick Jongert,\nMarc Lievens, Jean-Louis Ruelle, Sandra Morel, Sophie Delhaye, and Robert\nVandermost—for their valued suggestions and guidance.\nReferences\n1. Stoute JA, Slaoui M, Heppner DG, et al. A preliminary evaluation of\na recombinant circumsporozoite protein vaccine against Plasmodium\nfalciparum malaria. RTS,S Malaria Vaccine Evaluation Group. N Engl\nJ Med 1997; 336:86–91.\n2. Garcon N, Heppner DG, Cohen J. Development of RTS,S/AS02: a\npuriﬁed subunit-based malaria vaccine candidate formulated with a\nnovel adjuvant. Expert Rev Vaccines 2003; 2:231–8.\n3. Heppner DG Jr, Kester KE, Ockenhouse CF, et al. Towards an RTS,S-\nbased, multi-stage, multi-antigen vaccine against falciparum malaria:\nprogress at the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research. Vaccine\n2005; 23:2243–50.\n4. Kester KE, McKinney DA, Tornieporth N, et al. Efﬁcacy of recombinant\ncircumsporozoite protein vaccine regimens against experimental Plas-\nmodium falciparum malaria. J Infect Dis 2001; 183:640–7.\n5. Kester KE, McKinney DA, Tornieporth N, et al. A phase I/IIa safety,\nimmunogenicity, and efﬁcacy bridging randomized study of a two-dose\nregimen of liquid and lyophilized formulations of the candidate malaria\nvaccine RTS,S/AS02 in malaria-naı¨ve adults. Vaccine 2007; 25:5359–66.\n6. Kester KE, Cummings JF, Ockenhouse CF, et al. Phase 2a trial of 0,1\nand 3 month and 0, 7 and 28 day immunization schedules of malaria\nvaccine RTS, S/AS02 in malaria-naı¨ve adults at the Walter Reed Army\nInstitute of Research. Vaccine 2008; 26:2191–202.\n7. Kester KE, Cummings JF, Ofori-Anyinam O, et al. Randomized, dou-\nble-blind, phase 2 trial of falciparum malaria vaccines RTS,S/AS01B\nand RTS,S/AS02A in malaria-naive adults: safety, efﬁcacy, and im-\nmunologic associates of protection. J Infect Dis 2009; 200:337–46.\n8. Mettens P, Dubois PM, Demoitie´ MA, et al. Improved T cell responses\nto Plasmodium falciparum circumsporozoite protein in mice and mon-\nkeys induced by a novel formulation of RTS,S vaccine antigen. Vaccine\n2008; 26:1072–82.\n9. Stewart VA, McGrath SM, Walsh DS, et al. Pre-clinical evaluation of\nnew adjuvant formulations to improve the immunogenicityofthemalaria\nvaccine RTS,S/AS02. Vaccine 2006;24:6483–92.\n10. Stewart VA, Walsh DS, McGrath SM, et al. Cutaneous delayed-type\nhypersensitivity (DTH) in a multi-formulation comparator trial of the\nanti-falciparum malaria vaccine candidate RTS,S in rhesus macaques.\nVaccine 2006; 24:6493–502.\n11. Gordon DM, McGovern TW, Krzych U, et al. Safety, immunogenicity,\nand efﬁcacy of a recombinantly produced Plasmodium falciparum cir-\ncumsporozoite protein–hepatitis B surface antigen subunit vaccine. J\nInfect Dis 1995;171:1576–85\n12. Sun P, Schwenk R, White K, et al. Protective immunity against induced\nwith malaria vaccine RTS,S is linked to Plasmodium falciparum cir-\ncumsporozoite protein-speciﬁc CD4+ and CD8+ T cells producing IFN-\ng. J Immunol 2003; 171:6961–7.\n13. Michiels S, Koscielny S, Hill C. Prediction of cancer outcome with\nmicroarrays: a multiple random validation strategy. Lancet 2005; 365:\n488–92.\n14. Hogan CM, Hammer SM. Host determinants in HIV infection and dis-\nease. Part 1: cellular and humoral immune responses. Ann Intern Med\n2001;134:761–6.\n15. Mistry R, Cliff JM, Clayton CL, et al. Gene-expression patterns in whole\nblood identify subjects at risk for recurrent tuberculosis. J Infect Dis\n2007; 195:357–65.\n16. Satoh J, Nanri Y, Tabunoki H, Yamamura T. Microarray analysis iden-\ntiﬁes a set of CXCR3 and CCR2 ligand chemokines as early IFNb-\nresponsive genes in peripheral blood lymphocytes I vitro: an impli-\ncation for IFNb-related adverse effects in multiple sclerosis. BMC\nNeurol 2006; 6:18.\n17. van de Vijver, He YD, van’t Veer LJ, et al. A gene-expression signature\nas a predictor of survival in breast cancer. N Engl J Med 2002; 47:\n1999–2009.\n18. Paik S, Shak S, Tang G, et al. A multigene assay to predict recurrence\nof tamoxifen-treated, node-negative breast cancer. N Engl J Med 2004;\n351:2817–26.\n19. Paik S, Tang G, Shak S, et al. Gene expression and beneﬁt of che-\nmotherapy in women with node-negative estrogen-receptor-positive\nbreast cancer. J Clin Oncol 2006; 24:3726–34.\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/201/4/580/863225 by Yale Library user on 26 June 2026\n\nDifferential Expression and Protection • JID 2010:201 (15 February) • 589\n20. Fan C, Oh DS, Wessels L, et al. Concordance among gene-expression-\nbased predictors for breast cancer. N Engl J Med 2006;355:560–9.\n21. Liu R, Wang X, Chen GY, et al. The prognostic role of gene signatures\nfrom tumorigenic breast-cancer cells. N Engl J Med 2007; 356:217–26.\n22. Foekens JA, Atkins D, Zhang Y, et al. Multicenter validation of gene\nexpression-based prognostic signature in lymph node-negative primary\nbreast cancer. J Clin Oncol 2006;24:1665–71.\n23. Gaucher D, Therrien R, Kettaf N, et al. Yellow fever vaccine induces\nintegrated multilineage and polyfunctional immune responses. J Exp\nMed 2008; 205:3119–31.\n24. Querec TD, Akondy RS, Lee EK, et al. Systems biology approach pre-\ndicts immunogenicity of the yellow fever vaccine in humans. Nat Im-\nmunol 2009; 10:116–25.\n25. Garc¸on N, Chomez P, Van Mechelen M. G